mode: "100644"
text: "# Arcs, specifics, platform framing + two worked examples\n\n## Arc structures (pick by goal)\n| Structure | Shape | Best for |\n|---|---|---|\n| **BAB** | Before (pain) → After (better) → Bridge (how) | offers, transformations, product value |\n| **3-Act** | setup → turning point → resolution | most posts, Reels, case studies |\n| **Hero's Journey** | call → struggle → return changed | founder/origin, longer video |\n| **StoryBrand** | customer-hero + brand-guide + plan → success | brand messaging, landing-page-style posts |\n\n## Specificity technique (vague → concrete)\n```\n\"We help businesses grow.\"            -> \"Last March a 2-person bakery messaged us at midnight, panicking about a 400-order weekend.\"\n\"I was overwhelmed.\"                  -> \"Shoulders tight, jaw clenched, staring at a dashboard at 2 AM, coffee gone cold.\"\n\"Customers love our product.\"         -> \"She texted a photo of the empty tray with one word: 'gone.'\"\nRule: one true, specific, sensory detail creates presence. Show the scene; don't summarise the feeling.\n```\n\n## Platform framing (same story, different framing)\n```\nTikTok:    personality + vulnerability; talk to camera; fast micro-arc; relatable > polished\nLinkedIn:  professional transformation / lesson learned; \"here's what we got wrong and what changed\"\nInstagram: visual-first; a 5-7 frame carousel = a complete arc (cover = setup, last = payoff)\nYouTube:   longer emotional arc; clearer 3-act; let tension build\nThreads/X: short written arc; one tension, one turn, one line of meaning\n-> Keep the narrative VOICE consistent across all of them (recognition > visual sameness).\n```\n\n## Serialized storytelling (compounds)\n```\nA 6-post launch series, a monthly \"what we got wrong\", a weekly customer-transformation -> anticipation + return visits.\nMap the arc in the content-calendar so it has a real beginning/middle/end (don't let it trail off after post 2).\n```\n\n## Metrics + WoopSocial flow\n```\nSTORY METRICS: saves, shares, comment length, profile visits -- NOT likes (\"40 likes/200 saves > 400 likes/3 saves\").\nFLOW: agent finds + structures the story -> format writer builds it (reels-script/carousel-writer/linkedin-post-writer) +\nhook-writer for the open -> visual via image tools/design-and-templates -> WoopSocial publishes (series mapped in content-calendar).\nWoopSocial does NOT generate the story or judge resonance.\n```\n\n## Worked example 1 - SaaS founder, LinkedIn (blunt indie-founder voice)\n```\nReal story: we shipped a feature nobody used. Arc (3-act): setup (we were proud of it) -> turning point (0 adoption, one brutal\nuser call) -> payoff (what we learned + the rebuild that worked). Specific: \"a user said 'I genuinely don't understand what this\ndoes' on a Tuesday call -- I muted and stared at the wall.\" Meaning: ship the problem, not the feature. linkedin-post-writer builds it. Metric I watch: saves + comments, not likes.\n```\n\n## Worked example 2 - bakery, Instagram carousel (warm studio voice)\n```\nA true customer moment, told as a 6-frame carousel. Frame 1 (setup): a midnight message before a 400-order weekend. Middle frames:\nthe scramble, the early mornings, the small fix that saved it. Last frame (payoff): the empty tray and a one-word text -- \"gone.\"\nThe hero is the customer, we're just the guide. carousel-writer lays it out, the cover does the hook. We watch saves + shares. Nothing invented -- it really happened, shared with permission.\n```\n\nBoth: a TRUE story; a clear arc with the customer as hero; one concrete sensory detail; one line of meaning; built by the\nright format writer; measured by saves/shares. Nothing fabricated.\n"
